For most of this review, we will measure distances using the norm ( 3 ). However, occasionally a mathematician may be interested in a variation on the theme of ( 5 ), where the Frobenius norm is replaced by a different matrix norm Byers (1988) , Gillis and Glineur (2011) , Higham (1988) . The spectral norm ∥ A ∥ 2 = σ max ​ ( A ) \lVert AVert_{2}=\sigma_{\max}(A) is an especially common alternative; see for example its use in the context of computing the distance to uncontrollability Gu et al. (2006) or for the nearest defective matrix problem Malyshev (1999) . Moreover, beyond norm-based distances, matrix nearness problems can also be formulated using Bregman divergences Dhillon and Tropp (2007) , which generalize the squared Frobenius distance. When the chosen norm is not induced by an inner product, this carries the big disadvantage of losing the real Hilbert space structure of 𝔽 m × n \mathbb{F}^{m	imes n} , and in particular we cannot recycle the orthogonality arguments that are used to obtain formulae for the Frobenius projection. For some problems, this disadvantage is tangible. For example, suppose that we seek a symmetric positive semidefinite matrix nearest to a real input matrix A A . In the Frobenius norm the solution can be computed by mapping to 0 0 all the negative eigenvalues, if any, of the symmetric part ( A + A T ) / 2 (A+A^{T})/2 ; but in the spectral norm, the matrix thus constructed may not be the nearest one to A A (see Example 2.18 below for a more detailed discussion), and Higham (1988) develops instead iterative methods based on a characterization due to Halmos (1972) . On the other hand, the Hilbert space structure does not by itself make a nearness problem easy to solve. For example, the weighted Frobenius norm given by ‖ X ‖ 2 = ∑ i , j ω i ​ j ​ | X i ​ j | 2 \|X\|^{2}=\sum_{i,j}\omega_{ij}|X_{ij}|^{2} , where the weights ω i ​ j \omega_{ij} are positive, is still induced by an inner product. Nevertheless, Gillis and Glineur (2011) prove that, for real matrices, computing a nearest matrix of rank ≤ 1 \leq 1 in the associated distance is generally NP-hard; a somewhat surprising result since this problem is relatively easy for a vast class of norms (see Example 3.14 below). Even in the Frobenius distance itself, some matrix nearness problems can be proved to be NP-hard Zhang et al. (2023) . It is clear that the minimizer, in general, depends on the chosen norm. We illustrate this fact in Example 2.1 and Example 2.2 . Example 2.1 . Let A = 0 A=0 and ℰ = { [ a 0 0 b ] , a , b ∈ ℝ , a + 2 b = 3 } \mathcal{E}=\left\{ egin{bmatrix}a&0\ 0&b\end{bmatrix},a,b\in\mathbb{R},a+2b=3ight\} . Table 2.1 displays minimizers of ‖ X − A ‖ \|X-A\| over X ∈ ℰ X\in\mathcal{E} for the Frobenius, spectral, and nuclear norms, which are all different. (Recall that the nuclear norm is the sum of the singular values.) Norm type min ⁡ ∥ X ∥ ​ s.t. ​ X ∈ ℰ \min\lVert XVert\ 	ext{s.t.}\ X\in\mathcal{E} [ 3 5 0 0 6 5 ]  egin{bmatrix} rac{3}{5}&0\ 0& rac{6}{5}\end{bmatrix} [ 1 0 0 1 ]  egin{bmatrix}1&0\ 0&1\end{bmatrix} [ 0 0 0 3 2 ]  egin{bmatrix}0&0\ 0& rac{3}{2}\end{bmatrix} Frobenius ∥ ⋅ ∥ F \lVert\cdotVert_{F} 3 5 ≈ 1.34  rac{3}{\sqrt{5}}\approx 1.34 3 5 ≈ 1.34  rac{3}{\sqrt{5}}\approx 1.34 2 ≈ 1.41 \sqrt{2}\approx 1.41 3 2 = 1.5  rac{3}{2}=1.5 Spectral ∥ ⋅ ∥ 2 \lVert\cdotVert_{2} 1 1 6 5 = 1.2  rac{6}{5}=1.2 1 1 3 2 = 1.5  rac{3}{2}=1.5 Nuclear ∥ ⋅ ∥ ∗ \lVert\cdotVert_{*} 3 2 = 1.5  rac{3}{2}=1.5 9 5 = 1.8  rac{9}{5}=1.8 2 2 3 2 = 1.5  rac{3}{2}=1.5 Table 2.1 : Minimizers for Example 2.1 in various unitarily invariant norms. The last three columns give the distance, induced by the norms specified in each row, from A = 0 A=0 to the matrices in ℰ \mathcal{E} displayed on top. Red colour highlights the global minima of each distance. Our second example highlights a surprising connection with some elementary results in Euclidean geometry. Example 2.2 . Let A ∈ ℂ 3 × 3 A\in\mathbb{C}^{3	imes 3} be diagonal matrix, such that