Interestingly, there are also some matrix nearness problems for which the same matrix is simultaneously a minimizer for the distances induced by many norms. In particular, there are several remarkable results in this sense if we restrict our attention to unitarily invariant norms. The theme of unitary invariance will reoccur later in our article, see Subsection 3.2 . Recall that a matrix norm ∥ ⋅ ∥ \|\cdot\| is unitarily invariant if ‖ A ‖ = ‖ U ​ A ​ V ‖ \|A\|=\|UAV\| for every pair of unitary matrices U , V U,V of suitable size. For example, the Frobenius norm ( 3 ) and the spectral norm are unitarily invariant. Following Bhatia (1996) , we typically assume that unitarily invariant norms are always normalized , i.e., ∥ e 1 ​ e 1 ∗ ∥ = 1 \lVert e_{1}e_{1}^{*}Vert=1 . A good amount of interest has been given in the literature to matrix nearness problems admitting a “unitarily universal” minimizer, that is, a matrix that solves the problem ( 5 ) for every unitarily invariant norm. Historically, this line of research can be traced back to the classic studies on low-rank approximation Schmidt (1907) , Eckart and Young (1936) , Mirsky (1960) , where an appropriate truncation of the SVD is a universal minimizer for unitarily invariant norms; indeed, statements about properties that hold in every unitarily invariant norm are also known as Mirsky-type results . In the context of nearness problems, there is a strong computational motivation. Indeed, sometimes an application dictates that a unitarily invariant norm other than the Frobenius norm (often the spectral norm Das and Bora (2020) , Gu et al. (2006) , Malyshev (1999) ) is the preferable choice to compute distances. If a unitarily universal minimizer exists and coincides with the unique Frobenius minimizer, the Hilbert space structure associated with Frobenius optimization can be exploited also to compute the different distance of interest. The following lemma gives a first noteworthy case. Lemma 2.7 . Let A ∈ 𝔽 m × n A\in\mathbb{F}^{m	imes n} , and ℰ ⊆ 𝔽 m × n \mathcal{E}\subseteq\mathbb{F}^{m	imes n} be a closed set. Suppose that X ⋆ ∈ arg ⁡ min X ∈ ℰ ⁡ ∥ X − A ∥ 2 X_{\star}\in\arg\min_{X\in\mathcal{E}}\lVert X-AVert_{2} is such that X ⋆ − A X_{\star}-A has rank 1 1 . Then, X ⋆ ∈ arg ⁡ min X ∈ ℰ ​ ∥ X − A ∥ X_{\star}\in\arg\min_{X\in\mathcal{E}}\lVert X-AVert for every unitarily invariant norm ∥ ⋅ ∥ \lVert\cdotVert . Proof 2.8 . We may suppose without loss of generality (up to rescaling) that ∥ ⋅ ∥ \lVert\cdotVert is normalized. For any normalized unitarily invariant norm we have ∥ M ∥ 2 ≤ ∥ M ∥ \lVert MVert_{2}\leq\lVert MVert for all matrices M M , with equality if M M has rank 1 ( Bhatia 1996 , (IV.38)) . With this result, the proof is simple: For every X ∈ ℰ X\in\mathcal{E} , ∥ X ⋆ − A ∥ = ∥ X ⋆ − A ∥ 2 ≤ ∥ X − A ∥ 2 ≤ ∥ X − A ∥ . \lVert X_{\star}-AVert=\lVert X_{\star}-AVert_{2}\leq\lVert X-AVert_{2}\leq\lVert X-AVert. Example 2.9 . Let A ∈ 𝔽 m × n A\in\mathbb{F}^{m	imes n} , and assume for simplicity that m ≥ n m\geq n and that A A has full column rank n n . By the Schmidt-Eckart-Young-Mirsky Theorem (Example 3.14 ), a nearest rank deficient matrix to A A in the spectral norm is X ⋆ = A − Δ ⋆ X_{\star}=A-\Delta_{\star} with Δ ⋆ = u n ​ σ n ​ v n ∗ \Delta_{\star}=u_{n}\sigma_{n}v_{n}^{*} , where u n , σ n , v n u_{n},\sigma_{n},v_{n} come from a (possibly non-unique) SVD of A A . Since the optimal perturbation Δ ⋆ \Delta_{\star} has rank 1, it is also a unitarily universal minimizer by Lemma 2.7 . Note also that X ⋆ X_{\star} is not the unique minimizer in the spectral norm: For instance, we can take any matrix of the form X ⋆ = U ​ diagm ⁡ ( τ 1 , τ 2 , … , τ n − 1 , 0 ) ​ V ∗ X_{\star}=U\diagm(	au_{1},	au_{2},\dots,	au_{n-1},0)V^{*} , where τ i ∈ 𝔽 	au_{i}\in\mathbb{F} with | σ i − τ i | ≤ σ n \lvert\sigma_{i}-	au_{i}vert\leq\sigma_{n} for i = 1 , 2 , … , n − 1 i=1,2,\dots,n-1 . But not all these matrices are unitarily universal minimizers. Another pioneering contribution is due to Fan and Hoffman (1955) , who showed that the (skew-)Hermitian part of a square matrix is the nearest (skew-)Hermitian matrix in every unitarily invariant norm. Theorem 2.10 ( Fan and Hoffman (1955) ) . Let A ∈ 𝔽 n × n A\in\mathbb{F}^{n	imes n} , and ℰ H = { X ∈ 𝔽 n × n : X = X ∗ } \mathcal{E}_{H}=\{X\in\mathbb{F}^{n	imes n}\colon X=X^{*}\} . Then, the Hermitian part A + A ∗ 2  rac{A+A^{*}}{2} is a minimizer of d ⁡ ( A , ℰ H ) d(A,\mathcal{E}_{H}) in every unitarily invariant norm. Proof 2.11 . Let X ⋆ = A + A ∗ 2 X_{\star}= rac{A+A^{*}}{2} , Δ ⋆ = A − A ∗ 2 \Delta_{\star}= rac{A-A^{*}}{2} be the (unique) decomposition of A A into a Hermitian matrix X ⋆ = X ⋆ ∗ X_{\star}=X_{\star}^{*} and a skew-Hermitian Δ ⋆ = − Δ ⋆ ∗ \Delta_{\star}=-\Delta_{\star}^{*} . Let A = X + Δ A=X+\Delta be any other decomposition with X = X ∗ X=X^{*} . Then, ∥ Δ ⋆ ∥ = ‖ A − A ∗ 2 ‖ = ‖ Δ − Δ ∗ 2 ‖ ≤ ∥ Δ ∥ + ∥ − Δ ∗ ∥ 2 = ∥ Δ ∥ . \lVert\Delta_{\star}Vert=\left\lVert rac{A-A^{*}}{2}ightVert=\left\lVert rac{\Delta-\Delta^{*}}{2}ightVert\leq rac{\lVert\DeltaVert+\lVert-\Delta^{*}Vert}{2}=\lVert\DeltaVert. This proof uses only two ingredients: the triangle inequality, and the fact that ∥ Δ ∥ = ∥ − Δ ∗ ∥ \lVert\DeltaVert=\lVert-\Delta^{*}Vert , which holds for every unitarily invariant norm because Δ ∗ \Delta^{*} can be obtained from Δ \Delta via multiplication by unitary matrices (this fact is easy to prove via the SVD). For this reason, the proof strategy can be extended to a more general setting. Theorem 2.12 . Let ℰ = B + 𝒱 \mathcal{E}=B+\mathcal{V} be a real affine space, where B ∈ 𝔽 m × n B\in\mathbb{F}^{m	imes n} and 𝒱 ⊆ 𝔽 m × n \mathcal{V}\subseteq\mathbb{F}^{m	imes n} is a real vector subspace. Suppose that the projection onto its orthogonal complement 𝒱 ⟂ \mathcal{V}^{\perp} can be written as Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( X ) = F 1 ​ ( X ) + F 2 ​ ( X ) + ⋯ + F k ​ ( X ) k , \Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(X)= rac{F_{1}(X)+F_{2}(X)+\dots+F_{k}(X)}{k}, where the F i ​ ( X ) F_{i}(X) are isometries, i.e., ℝ \mathbb{R} -linear operators that preserve unitarily invariant norms. Then, the Frobenius norm projection X ⋆ = A − Δ ⋆ X_{\star}=A-\Delta_{\star} , where Δ ⋆ = Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( A − B ) \Delta_{\star}=\Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(A-B) , is a minimizer of d ⁡ ( A , ℰ ) d(A,\mathcal{E}) in every unitarily invariant norm. Proof 2.13 . The proof is a direct extension of the one of Theorem 2.10 , which is the special case F 1 ​ ( X ) = X F_{1}(X)=X , F 2 ​ ( X ) = − X ∗ F_{2}(X)=-X^{*} , B = 0 B=0 . Let A = X + Δ A=X+\Delta be another decomposition of A A with X ∈ ℰ X\in\mathcal{E} . Then, ∥ Δ ⋆ ∥ \displaystyle\lVert\Delta_{\star}Vert = ∥ Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( A − B ) ∥ = ∥ Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( X − B ) + Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( Δ ) ∥ = ∥ Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( Δ ) ∥ \displaystyle=\lVert\Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(A-B)Vert=\lVert\Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(X-B)+\Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(\Delta)Vert=\lVert\Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(\Delta)Vert ≤ ∥ F 1 ​ ( Δ ) ∥ + ⋯ + ∥ F k ​ ( Δ ) ∥ k = ∥ Δ ∥ . \displaystyle\leq rac{\lVert F_{1}(\Delta)Vert+\dots+\lVert F_{k}(\Delta)Vert}{k}=\lVert\DeltaVert. This averaging strategy, related to pinching , is classic in matrix analysis; see for instance Bhatia (1996, Section IV.2) , Bhatia (2000) . Usually the family of maps { F i } \{F_{i}\} has some structure, such as being a matrix group. More minimizers can be proved to be unitarily universal using this strategy. Theorem 2.14 . Let A ∈ 𝔽 n × n A\in\mathbb{F}^{n	imes n} . Then, in every unitarily invariant norm: 1. A − A ∗ 2  rac{A-A^{*}}{2} is a skew-Hermitian matrix closest to A A Fan and Hoffman (1955) ; 2. A − diagm ⁡ ( A 11 , … , A n ​ n ) A-\diagm(A_{11},\dots,A_{nn}) is a zero-diagonal matrix closest to A A (cfr. Bhatia (1996, Problem II.5.5) , Bhatia (2000) ); 3. A − tr ⁡ ( A ) n ​ I n A- rac{	raccia(A)}{n}I_{n} is a trace-zero matrix closest to A A Ziȩtak (1996) . Proof 2.15 . Item 1 follows from taking Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( X ) = X + X ∗ 2 \Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(X)= rac{X+X^{*}}{2} . Item 2 follows from taking Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( X ) = 1 n ​ ∑ k = 0 n − 1 Z k ​ X ​ ( Z k ) ∗ \Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(X)= rac{1}{n}\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}Z^{k}X(Z^{k})^{*} , where Z = diagm ⁡ ( 1 , ω , … , ω n − 1 ) Z=\diagm(1,\omega,\dots,\omega^{n-1}) , ω \omega being a primitive n n -th root of unity. Item 3 follows from taking Π 𝒱 ⟂ ​ ( X ) = 1 n 2 ​ ∑ k , ℓ = 0 n − 1 S ℓ ​ Z k ​ X ​ ( Z k ) ∗ ​ ( S ℓ ) ∗ , \Pi_{\mathcal{V}^{\perp}}(X)= rac{1}{n^{2}}\sum_{k,\ell=0}^{n-1}S^{\ell}Z^{k}X(Z^{k})^{*}(S^{\ell})^{*}, where S S is the cyclic shift matrix. Another classic application where this strategy appears is projecting a Hermitian A A onto positive semidefinite matrices. In the following, we use the Löwner order on Hermitian matrices: A ⪯ B A\preceq B means B − A B-A is positive semidefinite. Note that 0 ⪯ A ⪯ B 0\preceq A\preceq B implies ∥ A ∥ ≤ ∥ B ∥ \lVert AVert\leq\lVert BVert for every unitarily invariant norm; this is a consequence of Ky Fan’s Domination Theorem ( Fan 1951 , Theorem 4) and Weyl’s monotonicity theorem ( Bhatia 1996 , Corollary III.2.3) . Theorem 2.16 . Bhatia and Kittaneh (1992) Let ℰ + = { X ∈ 𝔽 n × n : X ⪰ 0 } \mathcal{E}_{+}=\{X\in\mathbb{F}^{n	imes n}\colon X\succeq 0\} . Let A ∈ 𝔽 n × n A\in\mathbb{F}^{n	imes n} be Hermitian, and let Q ​ diagm ⁡ ( λ 1 , … , λ n ) ​ Q ∗ Q\diagm(\lambda_{1},\dots,\lambda_{n})Q^{*} be an eigenvalue decomposition. Then, the positive part X ⋆ = Q diagm ( μ 1 , … , μ n ) Q ∗ , μ i = max ( λ i , 0 ) ∀ i = 1 , … , n , X_{\star}=Q\diagm(\mu_{1},\dots,\mu_{n})Q^{*},\quad\mu_{i}=\max(\lambda_{i},0)\  orall\ i=1,\dots,n, is a minimizer of d ⁡ ( A , ℰ + ) d(A,\mathcal{E}_{+}) . Proof 2.17 . Let Δ ⋆ = A − X ⋆ ⪯ 0 \Delta_{\star}=A-X_{\star}\preceq 0 , and let A = X + Δ A=X+\Delta be another decomposition with X ⪰ 0 X\succeq 0 . Let P P be the orthogonal projector onto the image of Δ ⋆ \Delta_{\star} , so that Δ ⋆ = P ​ A ​ P \Delta_{\star}=PAP . Then, ∥ Δ ⋆ ∥ ≤ ∥ Δ ⋆ − P ​ X ​ P ∥ = ∥ P ⁡ ( A − X ) ​ P ∥ ≤ ∥ A − X ∥ = ∥ Δ ∥ , \lVert\Delta_{\star}Vert\leq\lVert\Delta_{\star}-PXPVert=\lVert P(A-X)PVert\leq\lVert A-XVert=\lVert\DeltaVert, where the first inequality holds because Δ ⋆ − P ​ X ​ P ⪯ Δ ⋆ ⪯ 0 \Delta_{\star}-PXP\preceq\Delta_{\star}\preceq 0 , and the second follows from averaging. Example 2.18 . The reader may be tempted to combine Theorems 2.10 and 2.16 , and conclude that the positive part of 1 2 ​ ( A + A ∗ )  rac{1}{2}(A+A^{*}) is a nearest positive semidefinite matrix to a non-Hermitian A A in every unitarily invariant norm. Remarkably, this is false. For instance, when A = [ 0 4 0 0 ] A= egin{bmatrix}0&4\ 0&0\end{bmatrix} , if we project A A first onto Hermitian matrices and then onto positive semidefinite matrices we get H = Π ℰ H ​ ( A ) = [ 0 2 2 0 ] H=\Pi_{\mathcal{E}_{H}}(A)= egin{bmatrix}0&2\ 2&0\end{bmatrix} and then X = Π ℰ + ​ ( H ) = [ 1 1 1 1 ] X=\Pi_{\mathcal{E}_{+}}\left(Hight)= egin{bmatrix}1&1\ 1&1\end{bmatrix} . This resulting matrix X X is a distance minimizer in the Frobenius norm Higham (2002) , but not in the spectral norm: ∥ A − 2 ​ X ∥ 2 < ∥ A − X ∥ 2 \lVert A-2XVert_{2}<\lVert A-XVert_{2} . See Halmos (1972) , Bhatia and Kittaneh (1992) for more analysis and a proof that this double-projection returns the correct minimizer when A A is normal. Sun (1993) proves that, if the target set ℰ \mathcal{E} is described by a certain class of linear matrix equations, then the Frobenius projection is a universal minimizer for all unitarily invariant norms. We record this result as Theorem 2.19 ; in its statement and proof, and throughout the paper, X † X^{\dagger} denotes the Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse of the matrix X X ( Golub and Van Loan 2013 , page 290) , whereas A ⊗ B A\otimes B denotes the Kronecker product of A A and B B ( Golub and Van Loan 2013 , Section 1.3.6) . Theorem 2.19 . ( Sun 1993 , Lemma 1.3) Let B ∈ ℂ p × m , C ∈ ℂ n × q , D ∈ ℂ p × q , A ∈ ℂ m × n B\in\mathbb{C}^{p	imes m},C\in\mathbb{C}^{n	imes q},D\in\mathbb{C}^{p	imes q},A\in\mathbb{C}^{m	imes n} . Define ℰ = { X ∈ ℂ